Issues.Body:
### Background and motivation

The `System.Drawing.Drawing2D.Matrix.TransformPoints` and `TransformVectors` methods are intended to transform the array of values in-place, and that's clearly what the code is doing. The `T[]` overloads even call the `ReadOnlySpan` overloads to do the work. But because of the mutation, the span overloads should take `Span`s, not `ReadOnlySpan`s.

It may also be worth doing a sweep through `System.Drawing` to see if anything else was transcribed incorrectly like this.

I'm seeing this in .NET 10 and .NET 11-rc1. I don't know when these APIs were originally added.

### API Proposal

```csharp
namespace System.Drawing.Drawing2D;

public sealed class Matrix
{
    // Remove or mark [Obsolete("Use the Span overloads", true)]
    // Also add [EditorBrowsable(EditorBrowsableState.Never)]
    public void TransformPoints(params ReadOnlySpan<PointF> pts);
    public void TransformPoints(params ReadOnlySpan<Point> pts);
    public void TransformVectors(params ReadOnlySpan<PointF> pts);
    public void TransformVectors(params ReadOnlySpan<Point> pts);

    // Add these, and also tag with [OverloadResolutionPriority(1)] if not deleting the ReadOnlySpan overloads
    public void TransformPoints(params Span<PointF> pts);
    public void TransformPoints(params Span<Point> pts);
    public void TransformVectors(params Span<PointF> pts);
    public void TransformVectors(params Span<Point> pts);
}
```


### API Usage

APIs would be consumed in the same way they are today. I'm guessing most uses are going through the array overloads or via implicit cast to `ReadOnlySpan` _from_ arrays or mutable `Span`s.

### Alternative Designs

_No response_

### Risks

Removing the methods risks breaking any code that was using them. At compile time it may be fine, but it will definitely break binary compatibility.

### Will this feature affect UI controls?

No

### .NET version

.NET 10, but regression likely introduced in .NET 9. The problem occurs when referencing System.Drawing.Common version 9.0 or later. It is not related to runtime version.

### Did it work in .NET Framework?

Not tested/verified

### Did it work in any of the earlier releases of .NET Core or .NET 5+?

Yes, when referencing System.Drawing.Common version 8.0. 

The problem might have been introduced with #10783 or #10784. Now, ``Save(Stream, ImageFormat)`` calls the internal ``CoreImageExtensions.Save``, which passes the native pointer to GDI+ without keeping the image alive. The ImageCodecInfo overload calls ``GC.KeepAlive(this)``.

### Issue description

Saving an image with `Image.Save(Stream, ImageFormat)` to a `MemoryStream` can leak native memory if nothing else references the image. The GDI+ bitmap is never freed, and a full GC with finalizers doesn't recover it. Disposing the image avoids the leak, and so does using the `Save(stream, codec, null)` overload instead.

A possible explanation: `Save(Stream, ImageFormat)` calls the internal [`CoreImageExtensions.Save`](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/blob/release/10.0/src/System.Drawing.Common/src/System/Drawing/Image.cs#L293-L297), which [passes the native pointer to GDI+](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/blob/release/10.0/src/System.Private.Windows.GdiPlus/System/Drawing/CoreImageExtensions.cs#L28) without keeping the image alive, while the `ImageCodecInfo` [overload](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/blob/release/10.0/src/System.Drawing.Common/src/System/Drawing/Image.cs#L302-L328) calls `GC.KeepAlive(this)`. If the image is finalized while GDI+ is still saving it, `GdipDisposeImage` could fail, likely with [`ObjectBusy`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/gdiplustypes/ne-gdiplustypes-status). Since `Dispose` [ignores the status](https://github.com/dotnet/winforms/blob/release/10.0/src/System.Drawing.Common/src/System/Drawing/Image.cs#L206-L216) and clears the handle, the native bitmap would be orphaned.

### Steps to reproduce

```c#
#:package System.Drawing.Common@10.0.12

using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Drawing;
using System.Drawing.Imaging;

long before = NativeMemory();

for (int i = 0; i < 200; i++) { SaveBitmap(); }
GC.Collect();
GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();
GC.Collect();

Console.WriteLine($"Leaked: {(NativeMemory() - before) / 1024 / 1024} MB");

// Nothing keeps the bitmap alive during Save, so it can be finalized while GDI+ is still saving it.
static void SaveBitmap() => new Bitmap(1500, 1500).Save(new MemoryStream(), ImageFormat.Bmp);

static long NativeMemory() => Process.GetCurrentProcess().PrivateMemorySize64 - GC.GetGCMemoryInfo().TotalCommittedBytes;
```

Save the code above as `SaveLeak.cs` and run it on Windows with the .NET 10 SDK:

```console
> dotnet run -c Release -p:TargetFramework=net10.0-windows SaveLeak.cs
Leaked: 277 MB
```

The reported amount varies between runs, but it is never close to zero.

**Expected:** close to 0 MB. After a full GC with finalizers, all bitmap memory is freed.
**Actual:** hundreds of MB of native memory are never freed.

Changing the first line to `#:package System.Drawing.Common@8.0.31` gives `Leaked: 2 MB` on the same runtime.

Release mode is required in order to reproduce the issue.
